            <section id="intro">
                <h1>Digital Forensics</h1>
                <div class="card">
                    <h3>Define Digital Forensic?</h3>
                    <p>Digital forensics is a specialized field of forensic science that involves the identification, preservation, extraction, and analysis of digital evidence. It is used in criminal investigations, cybersecurity incidents, corporate fraud cases, and legal disputes where digital data plays a role.</p>
                </div>
                
                <h3>Why is Digital Forensics Important?</h3>
                <p>With the widespread use of computers, smartphones, and digital communication platforms, digital crimes have increased significantly. Digital forensics helps in:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Recovering Deleted or Altered Data:</strong> Using forensic tools to retrieve lost information.</li>
                    <li><strong>Investigating Cybercrimes:</strong> Tracking down hackers, fraudsters, and cyber terrorists.</li>
                    <li><strong>Providing Evidence in Court:</strong> Ensuring that electronic evidence is legally admissible.</li>
                    <li><strong>Ensuring Cybersecurity:</strong> Identifying and mitigating security breaches in corporate and government networks.</li>
                </ul>

                <h3>History of Computer Forensics</h3>
                <ol>
                    <li><strong>Early Beginnings:</strong> The roots of computer forensics can be traced back to the 1970s and 1980s when law enforcement and military investigators began to notice the increasing use of computers in criminal activities.</li>
                    <li><strong>First Recognized Computer Crime:</strong> In 1978, the Florida Computer Crime Act was enacted, recognizing the first computer crime.</li>
                    <li><strong>Development of Forensic Tools:</strong> Throughout the 1980s and 1990s, various tools and techniques were developed. This period saw the establishment of the first FBI Regional Computer Forensic Laboratory in 2000.</li>
                    <li><strong>Formation of Organizations:</strong> The International Organization on Computer Evidence (IOCE) was formed in 1995 to develop international standards.</li>
                    <li><strong>Academic Recognition:</strong> The term "Computer Forensics" was first used in academic literature in 1992.</li>
                    <li><strong>Modern Era:</strong> Today, computer forensics is a sophisticated and essential tool for law enforcement and cybersecurity professionals.</li>
                </ol>

                <div class="card">
                    <h3>Locard's Principle of Exchange in Digital Forensics</h3>
                    <p>Locard's Principle of Exchange states that <em>"every contact leaves a trace."</em> This principle is equally applicable in digital forensics, where every digital interaction leaves behind traces of evidence. For example, when a hacker infiltrates a computer system, they leave behind footprints in system logs and network traffic. Similarly, when a user deletes files, remnants often remain in storage media, making them recoverable. This principle also applies to metadata analysis, where timestamps, file access history, and user logs provide crucial evidence.</p>
                </div>
