                <h2>1. Legal Process Statutory Overview</h2>
                <p class="editable-field" contenteditable="true">
                    Law enforcement investigations in India follow strict legal procedures to ensure the absolute fairness, procedural integrity, and legal admissibility of all collected evidence before judicial trials.
                </p>

                <div class="transition-banner">
                    <strong>Statutory Framework Transition Notification</strong>
                    <span class="editable-field" contenteditable="true">As of July 1, 2024, India officially transitioned from its colonial-era criminal laws to a modern, technologically advanced legal framework. The Criminal Procedure Code (CrPC), 1973 has been replaced by the Bharatiya Nagarik Suraksha Sanhita (BNSS), 2023, and the Indian Evidence Act, 1872 has been replaced by the Bharatiya Sakshya Adhiniyam (BSA), 2023.</span>
                </div>

                <div class="statute-box">
                    <h3>a) Bharatiya Nagarik Suraksha Sanhita (BNSS), 2023</h3>
                    <div class="statute-meta" contenteditable="true">Successor Statute to the Code of Criminal Procedure (CrPC), 1973</div>
                    <p class="editable-field" contenteditable="true">
                        The BNSS establishes a comprehensive and thoroughly modernized framework for conducting arrests, executing search operations, executing property/asset seizures, and regulating criminal trials, explicitly integrating digital workflows and mandatory forensic interventions directly into day-to-day policing.
                    </p>
                    <div class="forensic-observation">
                        <strong>Forensic Observation: Section 105 Requirement</strong>
                        <span class="editable-field" contenteditable="true">Section 105 of the BNSS mandates that audio-video recording of search and seizure operations (including the preparation of the seizure list) via electronic devices is now compulsory. This drastically minimizes the risk of evidence tampering or staging at a crime scene.</span>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="statute-box">
                    <h3>b) Bharatiya Sakshya Adhiniyam (BSA), 2023</h3>
                    <div class="statute-meta" contenteditable="true">Successor Statute to the Indian Evidence Act, 1872</div>
                    <p class="editable-field" contenteditable="true">
                        The BSA fundamentally broadens the strict statutory definition of a "document" to comprehensively include electronic and digital records, ensuring that all digital data strands stand on completely equal legal footing with legacy physical documentation.
                    </p>
                    <div class="forensic-observation">
                        <strong>Forensic Observation: Admissibility Mandate</strong>
                        <span class="editable-field" contenteditable="true">Under the BSA, digital evidence is explicitly admissible, provided it is accompanied by a modified structural certificate (the successor to the old Sec 65B certificate) to prove that the computer system or device housing the data was operating correctly at the time of creation.</span>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </div>

