                <h3>2. Legal and Ethical Considerations</h3>
                <ul>
                    <li>Investigators must uphold the constitutional rights of suspects and witnesses, such as the right against self-incrimination and the right to legal representation.</li>
                    <li>Proper documentation of each step taken during the investigation is essential to demonstrate transparency, accountability, and legality.</li>
                </ul>

                <h3>3. Investigation Process</h3>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Scene Examination:</strong> Investigators carefully examine the crime scene to collect relevant evidence (physical objects, biological samples, electronic devices, documents).</li>
                    <li><strong>Interviews and Interrogations:</strong> Officers conduct interviews with victims and witnesses and may interrogate suspects in accordance with legal safeguards.</li>
                    <li><strong>Forensic Analysis:</strong> Applying forensic science techniques such as digital data extraction, fingerprint analysis, toxicology, or DNA testing.</li>
                    <li><strong>Report Filing:</strong> A comprehensive report (charge sheet) is prepared and submitted to the court, outlining findings and supporting evidence.</li>
                </ul>

                <h3>Role of an Investigator in Digital Forensics</h3>
                <p>A digital forensic investigator plays a crucial role in identifying, collecting, analyzing, and presenting digital evidence. The investigator must have a strong understanding of forensic tools, data recovery techniques, and legal procedures. Primary responsibilities include identifying and preserving evidence, ensuring digital artifacts remain unaltered, performing data analysis, and providing expert testimony in court.</p>

                <h3>Investigation Process in Digital Forensics</h3>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Identification and Collection:</strong> Identify and seize digital evidence (hard drives, mobile devices, cloud storage, network logs). Adhere to forensic best practices to avoid altering original data.</li>
                    <li><strong>Preservation:</strong> Create an image of the original data using tools like FTK Imager, EnCase, and Autopsy. Use hashing algorithms such as SHA-256 to maintain data integrity.</li>
                    <li><strong>Analysis:</strong> Use digital forensic tools to recover deleted files, analyze logs, decrypt data, and trace cybercriminal activities (email headers, browser history, network traffic, malware).</li>
                    <li><strong>Documentation and Reporting:</strong> Meticulously document every step. Prepare a detailed forensic report summarizing methods, evidence recovered, and conclusions.</li>
                    <li><strong>Court Presentation:</strong> Testify as expert witnesses, presenting forensic evidence and explaining its significance.</li>
                </ul>

                <div class="card">
                    <h3>Computer Forensics vs. Cyber Forensics</h3>
                    <p><strong>Computer Forensics:</strong> A subset of digital forensics focusing specifically on investigating computer systems, hard drives, file storage systems, and operating systems. Commonly used for investigating unauthorized access, cybercrimes, and recovering evidence from computer devices.</p>
                    <p><strong>Cyber Forensics:</strong> A specialized field dealing with crimes committed over the internet, such as hacking, phishing, online fraud, and cyber espionage. Focuses on tracking online activity, IP addresses, social media footprints, email analysis, and cybercrime investigations.</p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <section id="acquisition">
                <h2>Evidence Acquisition</h2>
                <p>The process of identifying, collecting, and preserving digital evidence from devices involves creating exact duplicates of the original sources. Data must stay unchanged and maintain a legally valid chain of custody for use in court.</p>
                
                <h3>The Three Golden Rules of Acquisition</h3>
                <ol>
                    <li><strong>Do not alter the original evidence:</strong> Work on duplicates, never the live source.</li>
                    <li><strong>Document everything:</strong> Record every tool, command, serial number, and timestamp.</li>
                    <li><strong>Maintain Chain of Custody:</strong> Chronologically log who handled the evidence, when, and why.</li>
                </ol>

                <h3>Acquisition Types</h3>
                <div class="table-container">
                    <table>
                        <tbody><tr>
                            <th>Type</th>
                            <th>Description</th>
                            <th>Pros</th>
                            <th>Cons</th>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><strong>Static (Dead) Acquisition</strong></td>
                            <td>Performed on a system that is powered down.</td>
                            <td>Safe, repeatable, prevents data from modifying itself via background OS tasks.</td>
                            <td>Misses unencrypted RAM data, running processes, and network connections.</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><strong>Live Acquisition</strong></td>
                            <td>Performed while the system is powered on and actively running.</td>
                            <td>Captures critical volatile memory (RAM), encryption keys, open network connections, and active chat sessions.</td>
                            <td>Modifies the system state slightly; risks triggering anti-forensic logic or data-wiping scripts.</td>
                        </tr>
                    </tbody></table>
                </div>

                <h3>Storage Formats</h3>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Raw Format (.dd, .img):</strong> A raw, sequential write of every sector. Fast to create, globally compatible, but does not embed metadata or compressed data.</li>
                    <li><strong>Advanced Forensic Format (AFF):</strong> An open-source format providing metadata inclusion and compression option extensions (.aff).</li>
                    <li><strong>Expert Witness Format (EWF / .E01):</strong> The standard corporate/proprietary format used by EnCase. It embeds system metadata, hashes, and supports compression.</li>
                </ul>

                <h3>Step-by-Step Acquisition Procedure</h3>
                <ol>
                    <li><strong>Secure and Isolate the Device:</strong> Disconnect from local network environments and Wi-Fi. If live acquisition, capture RAM first.</li>
                    <li><strong>Apply Write-Blocking Hardware:</strong> Connect target media to a physical write-blocker or activate a software write-blocker to intercept modification commands.</li>
                    <li><strong>Select and Run Forensic Imaging Software:</strong> Launch an industry-accepted utility (e.g., FTK Imager, dd, dc3dd) to compute a source hash and begin copying bit-by-bit.</li>
                    <li><strong>Verify and Compute Destination Hash:</strong> The utility auto-computes the hash of the clone file. Compare it mathematically against the source disk hash. Record both in the case log.</li>
                </ol>
            </section>

            <section id="hashing">
                <h2>Hashing &amp; Hash Verification</h2>
                <p>Hashing is the process of generating a fixed-length string from any data input. In forensics, it is used to validate data integrity and identify known files. Even the smallest change in the source data results in a drastically different hash value.</p>
                
                <h3>Common Hash Algorithms</h3>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>MD5:</strong> Produces 128-bit hash. Fast, but susceptible to collisions.</li>
                    <li><strong>SHA-1:</strong> Outputs a 160-bit hash, slightly more secure than MD5 but deprecated due to vulnerabilities.</li>
                    <li><strong>SHA-256:</strong> Produces a 256-bit hash. Currently the gold standard for digital forensics.</li>
                </ul>

