                <h3>The 4 Pillars of Has
                    <li><strong>Fixed Output Length:</strong> Regardless of input size, the resulting hash length is always identical for that specific algorithm.</li>
                    <li><strong>Deterministic:</strong> The exact same input will always produce the exact same output hash.</li>
                    <li><strong>One-Way (Irreversible):</strong> It is mathematically impossible to reverse-engineer the original data using only the output hash value.</li>
                    <li><strong>Collision Resistant:</strong> Two completely different inputs must never produce the identical output hash.</li>
                
                
            

            <section id="preservation">
                <h2>Digital Evidence Preservation</h2>
                <p>Digital Evidence Preservation is the process of protecting and maintaining the integrity of digital data from the moment it is identified until it is presented in a court of law. Because digital evidence is "latent" and "volatile", preservation is the most legally sensitive phase.</p>
                
                <h3>The Core Objective: Integrity &amp; Authenticity</h3>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Physical Preservation:</strong> Protecting the hardware from damage, static, or remote signals.</li>
                    <li><strong>Electronic Preservation:</strong> Using mathematical proofs to ensure not a single bit has been altered.</li>
                </ul>

                <div class="card">
                    <h3>The "Golden Rule" of Preservation</h3>
                    <p><em>"Never perform an investigation or analysis on the original evidence media."</em> Instead, create a forensic image (bit-by-bit copy) and preserve the original in a secure, write-protected environment.</p>
                </div>

                <h3>Signal Isolation</h3>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Faraday Bags:</strong> Specialized pouches lined with conductive material that block all radio frequencies (Cellular, Wi-Fi, Bluetooth).</li>
                    <li><strong>Airplane Mode:</strong> A software-level isolation (risky, as it requires interacting with the live device).</li>
                </ul>

                <h3>Preservation Formulas &amp; Verification</h3>
                <p>Digital preservation relies on Validation. You prove preservation by matching the hash values at different stages:</p>
                <p style="text-align: center; font-weight: bold; font-size: 1.2rem; color: var(--accent-primary);">
                    Hash (Original Device) = Hash (Forensic Image) = Hash (Working Copy)
                </p>
                <p>If Hash_A ≠ Hash_B, the integrity has been compromised, and the evidence may be thrown out of court.</p>
            </section>

            <section id="chain-of-custody">
                <h2>Chain of Custody</h2>
                <p>The Chain of Custody is a strict, legally binding log in forensic investigations. It tracks every encounter with digital or physical evidence from start to finish. If investigators mess up the Chain of Custody, the defense might argue the evidence was messed with or contaminated, making it inadmissible.</p>

                <h3>Core Principles: The 5 Essential Questions</h3>
                <ol>
                    <li><strong>Who</strong> handled the device or file? (Full name and signature)</li>
                    <li><strong>When</strong> did the interaction occur? (Precise date and timestamp)</li>
                    <li><strong>What</strong> specific actions were performed? (e.g., dynamic acquisition, hash verification)</li>
                    <li><strong>Why</strong> was the interaction necessary? (e.g., moving to secondary secure storage)</li>
                    <li><strong>Where</strong> is the evidence currently located? (e.g., Safe Cabinet B, Lab Vault 3)</li>
                </ol>

                <div class="diagram-box">
                    [ Collection / Seizure ] → [ Transportation ] → [ Intake / Secure Storage ] → [ Analytical Lab Testing ] → [ Reporting ] → [ Court Presentation ]
                </div>

                <h3>Examples of Breaches</h3>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Physical Gap:</strong> An investigator leaves a seized laptop on their desk overnight instead of a temporary lockbox. Anyone could have accessed it.</li>
                    <li><strong>Documentation Failure:</strong> An analyst hands an .E01 image file to a senior researcher on a flash drive. Neither logs the exchange.</li>
                </ul>

