            <section id="imaging">
                <h2>Forensic Imaging &amp; Bit-by-Bit Copy</h2>
                
                <h3>Forensic Imaging</h3>
                <p>The process of creating a bit-stream duplicate (a sector-by-sector, bit-for-bit clone) of a physical storage medium. Unlike standard file copying, imaging captures the entire physical volume, including active files, deleted data, slack space, and unallocated clusters.</p>
                
                <h4>Hidden Data Captured by Forensic Imaging</h4>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Slack Space:</strong> The unused space left over at the end of a physical disk sector when a file does not take up the full allocated cluster. Often contains fragments of older, overwritten files.</li>
                    <li><strong>Unallocated Space:</strong> Areas of the disk where data has been deleted and marked by the OS as "available to write over." The underlying data remains intact until new data replaces it.</li>
                </ul>

                <h3>Bit-by-Bit Copy (Physical Image)</h3>
                <p>Involves creating a copy of all bits (1 or 0) from the original storage media to the target media in an accurate sequence, right from Sector 0 to the last sector. A normal logical copy is created in accordance with file system restrictions, whereas a bit-by-bit copy does not involve such limitations.</p>

                <div class="table-container">
                    <table>
                        <tbody><tr>
                            <th>Feature</th>
                            <th>Bit-by-Bit Copy (Physical Image)</th>
                            <th>Logical File Copy (Standard Backup)</th>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td>Data Captured</td>
                            <td>100% of data (Allocated + Unallocated + Slack space)</td>
                            <td>Only active, allocated files</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td>File System Awareness</td>
                            <td>Completely independent; copies raw data blocks</td>
                            <td>Dependent on the OS to read files</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td>Hidden &amp; Deleted Files</td>
                            <td>Fully extracted and preserved</td>
                            <td>Completely ignored</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td>Metadata Integrity</td>
                            <td>Preserves all system metadata, timestamps, and logs</td>
                            <td>Alters file timestamps (e.g., "Last Accessed")</td>
                        </tr>
                    </tbody></table>
                </div>

                <h3>Professional Tools for Bit-by-Bit Copying</h3>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Command-Line (Linux):</strong> <code>dd</code> (Data Duplicator), <code>dc3dd</code> (enhanced version with on-the-fly hashing), <code>dcfldd</code> (supports simultaneous hashing).</li>
                    <li><strong>GUI-Based:</strong> FTK Imager (AccessData), EnCase, Guymager.</li>
                </ul>
            </section>

            <section id="live-dead">
                <h2>Live vs. Dead Forensics</h2>
                
                <details open="">
                    <summary>Live Forensics</summary>
                    <div class="content">
                        <p>The process that involves detection, extraction, and analysis of digital evidence from a computer system that is still running and whose volatile memory is in its complete form.</p>
                        <h4>Observer Effect (Forensic Artifact Contamination)</h4>
                        <p>A core limitation stating that any tool executed on a live machine inevitably modifies that machine's state, overwriting unallocated memory blocks and altering file access logs.</p>
                        <h4>Driving Factors for Live Extraction</h4>
                        <ol>
                            <li><strong>Full Disk Encryption (FDE):</strong> If you pull the power plug, encryption keys evaporate from RAM, leaving the drive unreadable.</li>
                            <li><strong>Volatile Malicious Code:</strong> Advanced cyber threats and rootkits reside strictly in running memory space, leaving zero footprint on physical drives.</li>
                            <li><strong>Real-Time Connections:</strong> Captures active network handshakes, unencrypted text chat sessions, and active user logins that vanish on reboot.</li>
                        </ol>
                        <h4>Order of Volatility (RFC 3227)</h4>
                        <div class="diagram-box">
                            [ REGISTER CACHE ] (Microseconds) → [ RAM ] (Seconds/Minutes) → [ NETWORK STATES ] (Minutes/Hours) → [ DISK CLONES ] (Days/Weeks)
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </details>

                <details>
                    <summary>Dead Forensics</summary>
                    <div class="content">
                        <p>The practice of identifying, collecting, and analyzing digital evidence from a computing system or storage medium that is completely powered off, inactive, or disconnected.</p>
                        <h4>Core Advantages</h4>
                        <ol>
                            <li><strong>Zero Observer Effect:</strong> The OS cannot execute background tasks or change metadata.</li>
                            <li><strong>Perfect Repeatability:</strong> Analysis can be replicated sector-for-sector by independent third parties.</li>
                            <li><strong>Deep Artifact Extraction:</strong> Allows comprehensive "data carving" across the entire physical disk.</li>
                        </ol>
                        <h4>The Dead Forensic Workflow</h4>
                        <ol>
                            <li>System Preservation (keep off or controlled shutdown).</li>
                            <li>Media Extraction (remove HDDs, NVMe SSDs, SD cards).</li>
                            <li>Write-Blocker Engagement.</li>
                            <li>Forensic Imaging (.dd, .E01).</li>
                            <li>Hash Verification.</li>
                        </ol>
                    </div>
                </details>
            </section>

            <section id="memory">
                <h2>Memory Forensics (Volatile Memory Analysis)</h2>
                <p>The branch of digital forensics that involves capturing and analyzing a computer's volatile memory (RAM). It targets operational runtime data to extract deep artifacts such as active processes, execution threads, open network sockets, unencrypted encryption keys, and remnants of malicious code.</p>
                
                <h3>What Makes Memory Forensics Unique?</h3>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Subverting Rootkits:</strong> Advanced malware can hide files from the OS API, but because code must execute in RAM, it cannot hide from a full hardware memory dump.</li>
                    <li><strong>Trawling for Cleartext Artifacts:</strong> OS temporarily decrypts credentials, passwords, and recovery bit-keys in RAM. Memory forensics allows extraction before they are cleared.</li>
                    <li><strong>Uncovering Fileless Malware:</strong> Modern malware executes directly in memory space using built-in administrative tools, leaving zero trace on traditional hard drives.</li>
                </ul>

                <h3>Key Memory Artifact Targets</h3>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Process Trees (pslist, pstree):</strong> Chronological mapping of active processes showing parent-child relationships.</li>
                    <li><strong>Network Sockets (netscan):</strong> Live capture of active network sockets, listening ports, and destination IP addresses mapping back to specific PIDs.</li>
                    <li><strong>DLLs and Handles (dlllist, handles):</strong> Lists showing every Dynamic Link Library loaded into a process's memory space.</li>
                    <li><strong>Kernel Modules &amp; Drivers (modules):</strong> List of loaded device drivers. Attackers frequently insert malicious, unsigned drivers into kernel spaces.<!-- </li-->
                </li></ul>
            </section>

            <section id="encryption">
                <h2>Encryption, Decryption &amp; Tools</h2>
                
                <h3>Encryption Methods</h3>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Symmetric Encryption:</strong> Uses the same key for both encryption and decryption (e.g., AES, DES). Fast, but key distribution is a concern.</li>
                    <li><strong>Asymmetric Encryption:</strong> Uses a key pair (public and private). Data encrypted with the public key can only be decrypted with the private key (e.g., RSA, ECC). Commonly used in digital signatures and secure email.</li>
                </ul>
                <p>When the decryption key is unknown, tools like <strong>Passware</strong> or <strong>John the Ripper</strong> are used to attempt password cracking through brute force or dictionary attacks.</p>

                <h3>Key Tools and Techniques</h3>
                <div class="table-container">
                    <table>
                        <tbody><tr><th>Tool/Technique</th><th>Significance &amp; Use</th></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>Write Blockers</strong></td><td>Hardware or software tools that prevent any write operations on a source drive, ensuring original data is not modified and remains admissible in court.</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>Data Recovery (File Carving)</strong></td><td>Reconstructs files from fragments based on file signatures (magic numbers) even if the file system's metadata is damaged. Also analyzes Slack Space and Unallocated Space.</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>Vulnerability Assessment</strong></td><td>Scans systems for weaknesses. Helps determine how an attacker gained access, vital for reconstructing the chain of events.</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>EnCase</strong></td><td>Comprehensive tool for acquisition, analysis, and reporting. Supports various file systems (FAT, NTFS, HFS+, EXT) and ensures legal admissibility.</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>Forensic Tool Kit (FTK)</strong></td><td>Known for advanced data analysis and visualization. Powerful indexing system for searching large datasets, hashing, and case management.</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>Autopsy</strong></td><td>Open-source digital forensics tool with a graphical interface. User-friendly, cross-platform, offers modules for timeline, internet history, and hash analysis.</td></tr>
                    </tbody></table>
                </div>
            </section>

            <section id="volatile">
                <h2>Volatile vs. Non-Volatile Data</h2>
                <div class="table-container">
                    <table>
                        <tbody><tr><th>Feature</th><th>Volatile Data</th><th>Non-Volatile Data</th></tr>
                        <tr><td>Power Dependency</td><td>Requires continuous electrical current.</td><td>Power independent.</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Primary Location</td><td>RAM, CPU Cache, Registers.</td><td>HDD, SSD, Flash Drives, ROM.</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Forensic Lifespan</td><td>Seconds to minutes (highly fragile).</td><td>Years (highly stable).</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Extraction Method</td><td>Live Acquisition (Triage tools).</td><td>Static Acquisition (Bit-by-bit via write-blocker).</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Forensic Value</td><td>Real-time context (passwords, active connections).</td><td>Historical context (deleted files, user logs).</td></tr>
                    </tbody></table>
                </div>

                <h3>Seizure Procedures</h3>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Volatile Memory Seizure:</strong> Assess power state. If on, proceed immediately. Avoid rebooting. Attach prepared USB with acquisition scripts. Dump RAM to external storage. Hash and document system time, open apps, and network status.</li>
                    <li><strong>Non-Volatile Memory Seizure:</strong> Isolate from network. Document serial numbers and power state. Use anti-static bags. Do not power on or interact until in a controlled forensic environment.</li>
                </ul>

                <h3>Extraction Techniques</h3>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Restore Deleted Files:</strong> Locate unallocated clusters, use file carving based on structure, handle fragments, and validate integrity.</li>
                    <li><strong>Password Cracking:</strong> Extract hash, create wordlists, apply Dictionary, Brute Force, or Hybrid attacks, and validate.</li>
                    <li><strong>Slack Space Analysis:</strong> Determine cluster size, calculate unused space within the last cluster, and read using sector-by-sector access.</li>
                </ul>
            </section>

            <section id="artifacts">
                <h2>Artifact Analysis &amp; Metadata</h2>
                
                <h3>Artifact Analysis</h3>
                <p>The forensic process of identifying, extracting, parsing, and interpreting digital artifacts to reconstruct the chronological timeline of events.</p>
                <div class="table-container">
                    <table>
                        <tbody><tr><th>Category</th><th>Primary Purpose</th><th>Key Forensic Value</th></tr>
                        <tr><td>System Artifacts</td><td>Created automatically by the OS.</td><td>Proves system configuration, connected hardware history, boot cycles.</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>User Artifacts</td><td>Generated by direct human interaction.</td><td>Establishes user intent, knowledge, and explicit file interaction.</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Application Artifacts</td><td>Written by third-party software.</td><td>Reveals communication records, download histories, web activity.</td></tr>
                    </tbody></table>
                </div>

                <h3>Windows Forensic Goldmines</h3>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Windows Registry:</strong> Massive hierarchical database. <code>USBSTOR</code> logs USB drive connections. <code>UserAssist</code> tracks executed programs.</li>
                    <li><strong>Prefetch Files (.pf):</strong> Prove an application was actually executed, logging runtime, frequency, and dependent files.</li>
                    <li><strong>LNK Files &amp; Jump Lists:</strong> Reveal original file path, volume serial numbers, and target file dates, even if the target file is deleted.</li>
                </ul>

                <h3>Metadata &amp; The MACB Timestamp Framework</h3>
                <p>Metadata is "data about data." The most heavily analyzed components are MACB Timestamps:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>M - Modified:</strong> Exact moment the content inside a file was changed and saved.</li>
                    <li><strong>A - Accessed:</strong> Last time a file was opened, read, or interacted with.</li>
                    <li><strong>C - Created:</strong> Timestamp showing when the file was first written to its current volume.</li>
                    <li><strong>B - MFT Born / Changed:</strong> Specific to Windows NTFS, tracks when the file's metadata record itself was changed (permissions, renaming).</li>
                </ul>
                <div class="card">
                    <strong>Collection Rule:</strong> Connect target media to a hardware write-blocker to guarantee that mounting the device cannot send an automated update command to the system's MACB tables.
                </div>
            </section>

            <section id="exif">
                <h2>EXIF Data</h2>
                <p>EXIF is a standard format that defines specifications for data stored within the header of a digital media file (JPEG, TIFF, WAV). It functions as an automatic logbook of hardware setups, environmental factors, and geospatial measures.</p>
                
                <h3>Key Forensic Data Elements</h3>
                <ol>
                    <li><strong>Hardware Identifiers:</strong> Camera manufacturer, smartphone model, unique serial numbers, firmware version.</li>
                    <li><strong>Technical Capture Settings:</strong> Shutter speed, ISO, aperture, lens type, focal length.</li>
                    <li><strong>Temporal and Spatial Markers:</strong> Precise date/time of creation and exact GPS coordinate data (Geotagging).</li>
                </ol>

                <h3>Practical Investigative Case Studies</h3>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Case 1: Alibi Verification:</strong> A suspect presents a photograph of a receipt. EXIF internal creation time reveals the photo was taken three days prior to the date printed on the receipt, destroying the alibi.</li>
                    <li><strong>Case 2: Location Extraction:</strong> An anonymous extortion image contains embedded GPS coordinates (Latitude: 23.5255, Longitude: 77.8186), pointing directly to a specific building in Vidisha, India.</li>
                </ul>
            </section>

            <section id="registry">
                <h2>Registry Analysis</h2>
                <p>The Windows Registry is an enormous hierarchical database employed by Microsoft Windows for low-level configurations, device drivers, system services, and user preferences.</p>
                
                <h3>The Five Master Root Keys</h3>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>HKEY_CLASSES_ROOT (HKCR):</strong> Handles file associations and object execution mappings.</li>
                    <li><strong>HKEY_CURRENT_USER (HKCU):</strong> Contains configuration profiles for the currently logged-in user.</li>
                    <li><strong>HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE (HKLM):</strong> Contains global, hardware, and software settings applied to the entire machine.</li>
                    <li><strong>HKEY_USERS (HKU):</strong> Contains all actively loaded user profiles.</li>
                    <li><strong>HKEY_CURRENT_CONFIG (HKCC):</strong> Stores temporary hardware profile information active during runtime.</li>
                </ul>

                <h3>High-Yield Forensic Artifact Keys</h3>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>UserAssist:</strong> Found in <code>NTUSER.DAT</code>. Tracks every graphical program executed by that specific user (obfuscated with ROT13, reveals execution count and precise time).</li>
                    <li><strong>Run / RunOnce:</strong> Found in <code>HKLM\SOFTWARE\Microsoft\Windows\CurrentVersion\Run</code>. Attackers copy files here to achieve persistence.</li>
                    <li><strong>USBSTOR:</strong> Found in <code>HKLM\SYSTEM\CurrentControlSet\Enum\USBSTOR</code>. Logs manufacturer, product description, unique hardware serial number, and exact connection timestamps of every USB device.</li>
                </ul>
            </section>

            <section id="logs">
                <h2>Log Analysis</h2>
                <p>Log files are machine-generated timestamped documents capturing actions, transactions, errors, and authentications in a linear chronological manner.</p>
                
                <h3>The Major Log Classifications</h3>
                <ol>
                    <li><strong>Operating System Logs:</strong> Core OS kernel activity (e.g., Windows Event Logs, Linux syslog). Track logons, privilege escalations, crashes.</li>
                    <li><strong>Application Logs:</strong> Generated by specific software (e.g., database queries, Apache/Nginx web server access logs).</li>
                    <li><strong>Network &amp; Security Logs:</strong> Produced by firewalls, routers, IDS, and Active Directory. Track data packet routing and blocked traffic.</li>
                </ol>

                <h3>Analysis Methodologies</h3>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Timeline Analysis:</strong> Sorting records from different sources chronologically.</li>
                    <li><strong>Correlation Analysis:</strong> Linking separate logs via a common identifier (e.g., matching a firewall block with a user account ID at the same timestamp).</li>
                    <li><strong>Anomaly Detection:</strong> Establishing a baseline of normal behavior and flagging deviations.</li>
                </ul>

                <h3>Standard Log Syntax Examples</h3>
                <div class="card">
                    <p><strong>Web Server Access Log:</strong><br>
                    <code>192.168.1.45 - - [08/Jun/2026:04:12:33 +0000] "GET /admin/login.php HTTP/1.1" 200 4502 "-" "Mozilla/5.0"</code><br>
                    <em>Reveals attacking IP, precise timestamp, targeted web page, and successful response code (200).</em></p>
                    <hr style="margin: 1rem 0; border-color: var(--border-color);">
                    <p><strong>Windows Event Logs (High-Yield IDs):</strong></p>
                    <ul>
                        <li><strong>Event ID 4624:</strong> Successful User Authentication/Logon.</li>
                        <li><strong>Event ID 4625:</strong> Failed User Authentication (brute-force detection).</li>
                    <li><strong>Event ID 7045:</strong> New System Service Installed (malware persistence flag).</li>
                    </ul>
                </div>
            </section>

            <section id="email">
                <h2>Email Forensics</h2>
                <p>Dedicated to the identification, collection, preservation, and analysis of email messages, headers, server logs, and attachments to authenticate the source and reconstruct the transmission pathway.</p>
                
                <h3>High-Yield Header Fields</h3>
                <div class="table-container">
                    <table>
                        <tbody><tr><th>Header Field</th><th>Forensic Value</th></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>Received:</strong></td><td>Most critical field. Records IP address and domain name of sending/receiving servers with UTC timestamps. Read from bottom to top.</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>Message-ID:</strong></td><td>Unique alphanumeric string assigned by the initial mail server. Can be cross-referenced with server logs.</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>X-Originating-IP:</strong></td><td>Often records the real, physical public IP address of the client that originally composed the message.</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>Return-Path:</strong></td><td>Specifies where bounced notifications are sent. Exposes spoofing if it does not match the "From" line.</td></tr>
                    </tbody></table>
                </div>

                <h3>Email Authentication Protocols</h3>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>SPF (Sender Policy Framework):</strong> Public text record listing authorized IP addresses allowed to send emails on a domain's behalf.</li>
                    <li><strong>DKIM (DomainKeys Identified Mail):</strong> Appends a cryptographic digital signature to verify the email body and header were not altered during transit.</li>
                    <li><strong>DMARC:</strong> Policy framework instructing receiving servers on what to do if an email fails SPF or DKIM checks (None, Quarantine, or Reject).</li>
                </ul>
            </section>

            <section id="mobile">
                <h2>Mobile Forensics</h2>
                <p>Deals with the identification, extraction, preservation, and analysis of digital evidence from mobile devices (smartphones, tablets, GPS units).</p>
                
                <h3>Mobile Phone Architecture</h3>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Hardware:</strong> System on Chip (SoC), NAND Flash Memory, RAM, Baseband Processor, SIM Card (stores IMSI, authentication keys, SMS), Sensors (GPS, accelerometer).</li>
                    <li><strong>Software:</strong> Bootloader, Operating System (Android/iOS), App Ecosystem.</li>
                </ul>

                <h3>Mobile Phone Crimes</h3>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Phone Phreaking:</strong> Manipulation of telecommunication systems (e.g., SIM card duplication, IMSI catchers/Stingray devices).</li>
                    <li><strong>Call Tampering:</strong> Unauthorized alteration of voice communication records, call forwarding, caller ID spoofing, deletion of call logs.</li>
                    <li><strong>Fake Hotspots:</strong> Rogue Wi-Fi access points to intercept login credentials and deploy malware.</li>
                    <li><strong>Wireless Hack Walkthrough:</strong> Unauthorized intrusion via Wi-Fi/Bluetooth (packet sniffing, session hijacking).</li>
                </ul>

                <h3>Forensic Acquisition Techniques</h3>
                <div class="table-container">
                    <table>
                        <tbody><tr><th>Extraction Type</th><th>Mechanism</th><th>Forensic Depth &amp; Value</th></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>Logical Extraction</strong></td><td>Communicates via standard OS API protocols (like a manufacturer backup).</td><td>Extracts active files, contacts, call logs, SMS. Misses deleted data.</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>File System Extraction</strong></td><td>Leverages software vulnerabilities or developer access exploits to read root directories.</td><td>Captures application databases, configuration files, hidden logs, third-party chat caches.</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>Physical Extraction</strong></td><td>Bypasses the OS entirely to read the NAND flash memory chip sector-by-sector.</td><td>Generates a true raw bit-stream image. Extracts deleted data, unallocated space.</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>Chip-Off / JTAG</strong></td><td>Advanced hardware extraction involving physically desoldering the flash memory chip.</td><td>Final resort for heavily damaged or destroyed hardware.</td></tr>
                    </tbody></table>
                </div>

                <h3>Tools Used in Mobile Forensics</h3>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Cellebrite UFED:</strong> Most widely used globally. Extracts all types of data, supports Android, iPhone, SIM, SD cards.</li>
                    <li><strong>MSAB XRY:</strong> Commonly used in law enforcement. Secure extraction, works on locked devices with permissions.</li>
                    <li><strong>Oxygen Forensic Detective:</strong> Strong in app data recovery, recovers cloud data and deleted messages (WhatsApp, Facebook).</li>
                    <li><strong>Magnet AXIOM:</strong> Useful for mobile + computer analysis. Shows chat data, browsing history, images with easy visualization.</li>
                </ul>
            </section>

            <section id="cloud">
                <h2>Cloud Forensics</h2>
                <p>An inter-disciplinary approach applying digital forensics concepts to cloud computing. Investigators cannot physically seize a server rack from a data center, as it would compromise the data privacy of other corporate tenants.</p>
                
                <h3>Shared Responsibility Model</h3>
                <p>Differentiates between layers of infrastructure controlled by the client vs. the Cloud Service Provider (CSP). This decides the limits of data access.</p>
                <div class="table-container">
                    <table>
                        <tbody><tr><th>Cloud Service Model</th><th>Investigator's Collection Capabilities</th><th>High-Yield Forensic Targets</th></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>IaaS</strong> (e.g., AWS EC2)</td><td>Maximum Control: Deep access to virtual OS layer.</td><td>Live memory captures, VM disk snapshots, host OS configurations.</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>PaaS</strong> (e.g., Heroku)</td><td>Medium Control: No OS access. Rely on application execution frameworks.</td><td>Application performance metrics, database query transactions, deployment logs.</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>SaaS</strong> (e.g., Microsoft 365)</td><td>Minimum Control: No infrastructure visibility. Gather via admin panels/APIs.</td><td>User access control logs (IAM), sign-in history, configuration audit trails.</td></tr>
                    </tbody></table>
                </div>

                <h3>The Cloud Data Collection Process</h3>
                <ol>
                    <li><strong>Containment &amp; Isolation:</strong> Update security groups or IAM roles to isolate the compromised VM from the production network, keeping it running to preserve memory.</li>
                    <li><strong>Storage Snapshotting:</strong> Trigger cloud-native storage APIs to generate an instantaneous, point-in-time cryptographic copy of the virtual hard drive.</li>
                    <li><strong>Log Harvesting:</strong> Pull global audit histories (e.g., AWS CloudTrail) to trace initial entry vectors.</li>
                    <li><strong>Immutable Preservation:</strong> Move gathered snapshots and logs into an isolated, central forensic account utilizing write-once read-many (WORM) configurations.</li>
                </ol>
            </section>

            <section id="malware">
                <h2>Malware Analysis</h2>
                <p>The scientific analysis of suspected binary or scripting code to understand its primary function, origin, execution method, effects, and IoCs (Indicators of Compromise).</p>
                
                <h3>The Four Stages of Malware Dissection</h3>
                <ol>
                    <li><strong>Basic Static Analysis:</strong> Examining the file without executing it (inspecting header metadata, searching for readable text strings, checking cryptographic signatures). Safe but can be bypassed by packers.</li>
                    <li><strong>Basic Dynamic Analysis:</strong> Executing the malware inside a monitored, isolated virtual container (sandbox) to observe live behavior (file system mutations, spawned processes, network handshakes).</li>
                    <li><strong>Advanced Static Analysis (Code Analysis):</strong> Loading the binary into a disassembler or decompiler to map out underlying assembly instructions without running it.</li>
                    <li><strong>Advanced Dynamic Analysis:</strong> Executing the file inside an interactive debugger. Analyst steps through code instruction-by-instruction, altering CPU registers to bypass anti-analysis checks.</li>
                </ol>

                <h3>Sterile Sandbox Pipeline</h3>
                <div class="diagram-box">
                    <div class="flow-step">HOST OS (Physical Machine - Completely Disconnected from Internet)</div>
                    <div class="arrow">↓ Host-Only Network ↓</div>
                    <div style="display: flex; gap: 1rem; justify-content: center;">
                        <div class="flow-step" style="flex: 1;">ANALYSIS WORKSTATION (VM)<br><small>(Runs x64dbg, ProcMon, Ghidra)</small></div>
                        <div class="flow-step" style="flex: 1;">NETWORK EMULATOR (VM)<br><small>(Runs INetSim / FakeNet to capture traffic)</small></div>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <h3>Key Target Artifacts &amp; Indicators (IoCs)</h3>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Host-Based IoCs:</strong> Custom registry keys for persistence, dropped temporary files, mutated system drivers.</li>
                    <li><strong>Network-Based IoCs:</strong> Specific Command and Control (C2) server domain names, hardcoded public IP addresses, unusual port configurations.</li>
                    <li><strong>Binary Metadata Strings:</strong> Legible text strings inside the file structure, such as raw IP addresses or API function import names (e.g., <code>VirtualAlloc</code>, <code>WriteProcessMemory</code>).</li>
                </ul>
            </section>

            <section id="timeline">
                <h2>Timeline Analysis</h2>
                <p>The forensic process involved in identifying, collecting, sorting, and correlating events based on timestamps from multiple digital evidence sources into one chronological chain.</p>
                
                <h3>Super Timeline</h3>
                <p>An advanced forensic timeline that does not rely only on file system timestamps. Instead, all time-stamped events from all potential sources (OS log files, registry modifications, browser history, prefetch execution) are compiled into one master record.</p>

                <h3>The Problem of Time Skew &amp; Synchronization</h3>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Time Zones:</strong> Systems may record logs in local time or UTC. Best practice dictates converting all extracted timelines to UTC.</li>
                    <li><strong>Clock Drift:</strong> Hardware clocks can drift. Investigators must compute the time offset against a trusted time source (like an NTP server).</li>
                </ul>

                <h3>Practical Timeline Forensic Analysis Example</h3>
                <div class="card" style="font-family: monospace; font-size: 0.9rem; background: #2c241b; color: #d4af37;">
                    <p>[2026-06-08 04:12:01 UTC] - SOURCE: Web Browser Cache - User 'j_smith' downloaded 'network_scanner.exe'</p>
                    <p>[2026-06-08 04:15:22 UTC] - SOURCE: Windows Prefetch - App 'network_scanner.exe' executed (Run Count: 1)</p>
                    <p>[2026-06-08 04:15:30 UTC] - SOURCE: Security Log (4624) - Successful Network Logon from IP 192.168.1.105 (Admin Privs)</p>
                    <p>[2026-06-08 04:18:45 UTC] - SOURCE: File System (MFT) - File 'confidential_data.zip' was CREATED (C)</p>
                    <p>[2026-06-08 04:22:12 UTC] - SOURCE: Security Log (1102) - The Audit Log was cleared by user 'j_smith'</p>
                </div>
                <p><strong>Forensic Takeaways:</strong> The timeline clearly establishes causality: download → execution → privileged network sign-in → data staging → anti-forensic log wiping. If the file system layer claimed the zip file was created in 2024, but the chronological flow shows it appeared right after a 2026 malicious login, the analyst exposes a <strong>timestomping</strong> metadata manipulation attempt.</p>
            </section>

            

        
    </h3></div>

